Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock
Problem: You're given an array prices where prices[i] is the price of a stock on day i. You want to maximize profit by choosing a single day to buy and a later day to sell. Return the max profit — if no profit is possible, return 0.
Example:
Input: prices = [7,1,5,3,6,4]
Output: 5
// Buy on day 1 (price=1), sell on day 4 (price=6), profit = 5

Brute Force approach
--------------------
public class BestTimeBuySellStock {
	public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {
        int maxProfit = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {
			for (int j = i + 1; j < prices.length; j++) {
				int profit = prices[j] - prices[i];
				if (profit > maxProfit) {
					maxProfit = profit;
				}
			}
		}
		return maxProfit;
    }
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		BestTimeBuySellStock solution = new BestTimeBuySellStock();
		int[] prices = { 7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4 };
		int maxProfit = solution.maxProfit(prices);
		System.out.println("Maximum profit: " + maxProfit); // Output: Maximum profit: 5
	}
}

Time Complexity: O(n^2) because the outer loop runs $n$ times and the inner loop runs roughly n times for each outer step. This will cause a "Time Limit Exceeded" error on large arrays on LeetCode.
Space Complexity: O(1) because it only uses a few integer variables for tracking.

Best approach
---------------

public class BestTimeBuySellStock {
	public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {
        int minPrice = prices[0]; 
        int maxProfit = 0;
        
        // Start loop from the second element (index 1)
        for (int i = 1; i < prices.length; i++) {
            if (prices[i] < minPrice) {
                minPrice = prices[i];
            } else if (prices[i] - minPrice > maxProfit) {
                maxProfit = prices[i] - minPrice;
            }
        }
        
        return maxProfit;
    }
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		BestTimeBuySellStock solution = new BestTimeBuySellStock();
		int[] prices = { 7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4 };
		int maxProfit = solution.maxProfit(prices);
		System.out.println("Maximum profit: " + maxProfit); // Output: Maximum profit: 5
	}
}

Time complexity: O(n) — single pass, exactly what we wanted.
Space complexity: O(1) — just two variables.

Best approach -2
-----------------

public class BestTimeBuySellStock {
	public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {
        int minPrice = Integer.MAX_VALUE; //Integer.MAX_VALUE is a constant in Java representing the largest possible value an int can hold: 2,147,483,647.
        int maxProfit = 0;
        
        // Start loop from the second element (index 1)
        for (int price : prices) {
            if (price < minPrice) {
                minPrice = price;
            } else if (price - minPrice > maxProfit) {
                maxProfit = price - minPrice;
            }
        }
        
        return maxProfit;
    }
	public static void main(String[] args) {
		BestTimeBuySellStock solution = new BestTimeBuySellStock();
		int[] prices = { 7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4 };
		int maxProfit = solution.maxProfit(prices);
		System.out.println("Maximum profit: " + maxProfit); // Output: Maximum profit: 5
	}
}
